ory and meditation, and above all a quest for a lost homeland, *A Place Within* begins with diary entries from Vassanji's very first wide-eyed trip to India in 1993, then moves on to accounts from his subsequent and obsessive revisits. An intimate chronicle filled with fantastic stories and unforgettable characters, [it] is rich with images of bustling city streets and contrasting Indian landscapes, from the southern tip of India to the Himalayan foothills, from the Bay of Bengal to the Arabian Sea. Here, too, are the amazing histories of Delhi, Shimla, Gujarat, and Kerala, and of Vassanji's own family, members of an ancient sect that draws on both Hunduism and Islam.","publicationYear":2008,"displaySummary":"From inside front cover: Part travelogue and description, part history and meditation, and above all a quest for a lost homeland, *A Place Within* begins with diary entries from Vassanji's very first wide-eyed trip to India in 1993, then moves on to accounts from his subsequent and obsessive revisits. An intimate chronicle filled with fantastic stories and unforgettable characters, [it] is rich with images of bustling city streets and contrasting Indian landscapes, from the southern tip of India to the Himalayan foothills, from the Bay of Bengal to the Arabian Sea. Here, too, are the amazing histories of Delhi, Shimla, Gujarat, and Kerala, and of Vassanji's own family, members of an ancient sect that draws on both Hunduism and Islam.","primaryTopic":"Oceans, Rivers & Water","subjectEvidence":{"primarySubject":"Society & Culture","confidence":"medium","method":"evidence_score","candidates":[{"subject":"Society & Culture","score":8,"evidenceCount":1}],"evidence":[{"source":"llm_classifier","rawLabel":"Society & Culture","mappedSubject":"Society & Culture","score":8,"confidence":"high","note":"The book explores personal narratives and cultural reflections within the context of India, making 'Society & Culture' the dominant subject with 'Travel & Place' as a releva